## Behaviour worth knowing

~> **Read this before using it in a rule.** The value changes when you change network, and a
connection can renumber on its own. A security group rule forces replacement, so a changed address
means the next plan deletes the rule and creates a new one. That is right for an administrative
allow-rule and a poor idea for anything a service depends on. It also means whoever ran the last
apply decides who has access, which is rarely right from CI; use an explicit CIDR there.

* **An IPv4-mapped address is unwrapped.** An address the API reports as `::ffff:192.0.2.10` comes
  back as `192.0.2.10`, with a `/32` CIDR.
